1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Senhor da Casa da Aveleda. Moço Fidalgo da Casa Real. Representante do título de condessa de Pangim . Fidalgos da Casa Real.
2 CONT
2 CONT __________
2 CONT
2 CONT Aveleda’s history began in 1671, when the earliest records available began to document the estate's agricultural and wine production. The estate was founded by the Guedes family, which still owns it today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Near the end of the 19th Century, the estate was transformed when Manoel Pedro Guedes da Silva da Fonseca – Mayor of Penafiel and Deputy to the Parliament – decided to leave his life in Lisbon and moved to the Aveleda estate. Ther
2 CONC e, he re-focused the estate’s production solely on winemaking and, defying the archaic habits of the region, he ordered all existing vines replanted using the Cordon method and divided each parcel by grape variety. He also added a
2 CONC  large and modern winery (with the capacity of over 300 barrels, quite a feat for that time!) and invested heavily in the winery and the winemaking process; these efforts were recognized by many national and international awards, 
2 CONC including a Gold medal at the Berlin exhibition (1888), in Paris (1889) and later in Seville (1929).
2 CONT
2 CONT His two sons. Fernando and Manuel continued their father’s legacy as wine producers. Through the harsh times of both World Wars and the depression, they worked relentlessly to keep the business afloat, and in 1946 their work paid 
2 CONC off when sales rose significantly and Brazil as well as other world markets opened for Aveleda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the same time, Fernando's son - Roberto Guedes - happened to meet Eugène Hélice, a French winemaker that was coming back from the Douro Valley by train. He offered his services to Roberto Guedes, who invited the Frenchman f
2 CONC or lunch. After a long conversation, Roberto hired Mr. Eugène Hélice and he started producing 30,000 liters of wine. That wine was served among friends, family, some oenologists and they all liked it. The wine was named Casal Garc
2 CONC ia, after the name of one of the vineyards in the estate. The peculiar packaging of a blue toned bottle and the deep blue label with a traditional lace (typical from the Minho region) gave an important contribute to its incredible
2 CONC  success. With the total control of the modern vineyard and the new techniques applied by the French winemaker, Casal Garcia became an immediate hit and has remained the flaship wine of Aveleda.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second half of the 20th Centurey was an important epoch for the creation of a brand new cellar, a vinification center, the mechanization of the bottling process, the building of large tanks, the acquisition of an alambic to di
2 CONC still wine and produce “bagaceira” were only some of the diverse investments held at Aveleda. It was also the time of the opening of the first gourmet shop in Oporto, under the name of Aveleda. This store was very important for th
2 CONC e success of the products originating from the estate and it was famous for the lovely décor of its window, displayed with colorful flower structures.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, Aveleda is considered to be one of the three major wineries in Portugal, selling its wines to over 90 countries in the world and the largest Vinho Verde producer. It is with pride that we continue to exist as a family-owned
2 CONC  company through all of its aspects.
2 CONT
2 CONT in, http://aveleda.portugalwines.org/aveleda-about.php
