3 STAE ME
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Sailboat builder, Boatmaker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} SOUTHWEST HARBOR — Thomas de Witt Cuyler Morris passed away with great peace in the loving presence of his family and caregivers at his home on Dec. 7, 2008, after a valiant and determined challenge with lung cance
2 CONC r. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom was born in Bryn Mawr, Penn. to Gerd Wiese and DeWitt Cuyler Morris on Nov. 20, 1940. He leaves his most beloved wife of 43 years Justine “Tina;” and the pride and joy of his life, his children DeWitt Cuyler Morris and DeWitt’
2 CONC s wife Cindy of Southwest Harbor; Helen Scott Morris Tirone and her husband Bradley of Freedom, Maine; and Lia Morris Siff and her husband Danie of Oakland, Maine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom was blessed with the great love of his seven grandchildren: Samuel, Sophia and Thomas Morris; August and Stella Tirone; and Owen and Abigail Siff. He leaves his loving sister Gerd Morris Grace and brother William B. Morris, bo
2 CONC th of New York City; along with his wonderful nieces and nephews, Ingrid C. Beeman, Charles Grace III, Melissa Grace and Gerd Grace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom attended numerous academic institutions starting with Episcopal Academy and then Valley Forge Military Academy and Chestnut Hill Academy, all in the Philadelphia area. He graduated from Babson Institute in Boston with a degree
2 CONC  in business administration. He served in the National Guard with distinction and was a life member of the First City Troop of Philadelphia, George Washington’s founding troop. He was a member of the Society Of The Cincinnati, New
2 CONC  York State and a member of the Cruising Club of America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Southwest Harbor, Tom was a three-term member of the Pemetic Elementary School board and served on the town warrant committee. He was also a regular, for many years, at the Deaconseat Restaurant’s infamous “round table” where h
2 CONC e enjoyed coffee and the camaraderie of fellow community members. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom settled with his wife Tina, married on Oct. 16, 1965 in Philadelphia, where Tom tried his hand at banking, marine insurance and finally as a shipping agent. It was always the water and sailing that Tom loved more then any clas
2 CONC sroom or office, and with a dream in his heart moved to Southwest Harbor, Maine in 1972 to live on the island where he spent his childhood summers in Northeast Harbor, sailing. He started Morris Yachts in 1972 in the backyard wher
2 CONC e he built his first boat, a Friendship Sloop for eternal friends James and Marsha Wilmerding of Northeast Harbor. Al Michaud, then of Manset was his first employee and, to become, great friend. Tina worked at Tom’s side, keeping 
2 CONC books and was his ever-present supporter. He collaborated with yacht designer Chuck Paine of Tenants Harbor in 1973, and together they forged a vision of beauty that has led Morris Yachts to be recognized as one of the most presti
2 CONC gious yacht builders in the country, winning numerous Boat of the Year Awards from Cruising World Magazine over the last decade. Tom’s son Cuyler joined Morris Yachts in 1995 and together they rode the waves of managing a family b
2 CONC usiness, expanding Morris Yachts Service Industry and increasing their boat-building capacity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom was a stalwart, independent and humble man of great integrity, careful thought and hard work. One of his favorite expressions was, “If there is a will, there is a way.” He gave infinite guidance and love not only to his childr
2 CONC en, but also to many people he befriended in his life. He was a devoted and loving partner in life to his wife Tina. He will be indescribably missed by his family who wish him “smooth sailing” and peace on his new journey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The understated Morris was not a native of Maine, but his connection to the Pine Tree State goes back more than a century. Beginning in the mid-1880s, his family began what was to become a long tradition of summer vacations in Mai
2 CONC ne. Tom was born in Philadelphia in 1940 and developed an early passion for sailing on the family’s A Class sloop, which his grandfather acquired in 1913. Five generations sailed the A Class number seven before the family donated 
2 CONC her to the Mystic Seaport museum in 1975 where she is on display.
2 CONT
2 CONT After an early career in business, Morris gave in to his love of sailing and moved to Southwest Harbor, Maine where he set up shop finishing fiberglass Friendship sloops built by Jarvis Newman. In 1972, he opened the doors of Morr
2 CONC is Yachts in a boat shop he built behind the family house on High Road in Southwest Harbor. Production of the Frances 26 designed by Paine began in 1974 and set the course for the company’s future.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the company’s slogans – “Each one an Owner’s Original” was backed up by Tom’s close involvement in the build process. As a result, Tom became close friends with his owners and was wined and dined at their homes wherever he 
2 CONC went. He enjoyed this immensely.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a boatbuiler, one need only step aboard a Morris to witness his prowess. Go through any Morris and you will see his many influences – no detail was too small a true mark of a consummate sailor - hand-holds where they should be,
2 CONC  wide, clutter-free decks, a well thought out galley. The only compliant one might have with him as a sailor is that he was light on the provisioning. He would show up for a delivery with a sleeve of saltines and a can of herring 
2 CONC and maybe a can of corn beef hash. All Morris crew knew to bring along extra eats when doing a delivery with Tom.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his son Cuyler became President of the company in 2001, Tom was able to spend more time sailing. In his new role as Ambassador-at-Large, he sailed the first Morris yacht S/V Escapade a Morris 46, to Scotland which also happen
2 CONC ed to be his first transatlantic. As the company’s official delivery skipper he has sailed both the Morris 38 and the new M42 up and down the Atlantic seaboard taking them to the various boat shows Morris attends. Last year, he to
2 CONC ok delivery of his very own Morris M42 and cruised it down to Charleston, SC last spring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom enjoyed nothing more than strolling through the Morris shop and talking with the guys, meeting and visiting with owners and sailing anywhere, any time under any conditions. His legacy will live on through Morris Yachts and all
2 CONC  those that knew and loved him.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was always the water and sailing that Tom loved more than any classroom or office, and with a dream in his heart moved to Southwest Harbor in 1972, to live on the island where he spent his childhood summers in Northeast Harbor,
2 CONC  sailing. He started Morris Yachts in 1972, in the backyard where he built his first boat, a Friendship Sloop for eternal friends, James and Marsha Wilmerding of Northeast Harbor. Al Michaud, then of Manset was his first employee 
2 CONC and to become, great friend. Tina worked at Tom's side, keeping books and was his ever-present supporter. He collaborated with yacht designer Chuck Paine of Tenants Harbor in 1973, and together they forged a vision of beauty that 
2 CONC has led Morris Yachts to be recognized as one of the most prestigious yacht builders in the country, winning numerous Boat of the Year awards from Cruising World Magazine throughout the last decade. Tom's son, Cuyler, joined Morri
2 CONC s Yachts in 1995, and together rode the waves of managing a family business, expanding Morris Yachts Service Industry, increasing their boat building capacity, and now employ a greatly appreciated and dedicated crew of 130. Tom wa
2 CONC s a stalwart, independent and humble man of great integrity, careful thought and hard work. Of his favorite expressions was, "If there is a will, there is a way." He gave infinite guidance and love not only to his children, but al
2 CONC so to many people he befriended in his life. He was a devoted and loving partner in life to his wife, Tina. He will be indescribably missed by his family, who wishes him "smooth sailing" and peace on his new journey
