1 NAME Adeline Louisa Maria de Horsey Countess of Cardigan and Lancastre
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 DEC 1824
2 PLAC King Street
2 ADDR
3 CITY near Berkeley Square
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 MAY 1915
2 PLAC Deene Park
2 ADDR
3 CITY Corby
3 STAE England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Adeline Louisa Maria, Countess of Cardigan and Lancastre (December 24, 1824, to May 25, 1915), was the second wife of English peer James Brudenell, 7th Earl of Cardigan and later the wife of the Portuguese nobleman
2 CONC  Don António Manuel de Saldanha e Lancastre, Conde de Lancastre. She was the author of scandalous memoirs, My Recollections, published in 1909, under the name Adeline Louisa Maria de Horsey Cardigan and Lancastre, though strictly 
2 CONC speaking she was not allowed by the rules governing the British peerage to join her former and current titles together. Her book detailed events and people coupled with gossip concerning the establishment of Victorian England. Aft
2 CONC er her marriage to the Earl of Cardigan in 1858, Queen Victoria had refused to have her at court because Cardigan had left his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adeline was born near Berkeley Square, London, the first child and only daughter of Admiral Spencer Horsey Kilderbee and his wife, Lady Louisa Maria Judith (daughter of John Rous, 1st Earl of Stradbroke). From 1832, her father too
2 CONC k the surname "de Horsey", after his mother's maiden name. Her younger brothers were Algernon Frederick Rous de Horsey and William Henry Beaumont de Horsey. She made her entry into society in 1842, and became engaged to Infante Ca
2 CONC rlos, Count of Montemolin, the Carlist claimant to the Spanish throne, in 1848.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rumours spread after she was frequently seen riding without a chaperone in the company of seventh Earl of Cardigan, who famously led the Charge of the Light Brigade at the Battle of Balaclava in 1854, and was a friend of her fathe
2 CONC r. The Earl was still married to the former Mrs. Elizabeth Tollemache Johnstone, whom Cardigan had married in 1826, after she was divorced by another army officer, Lt. Col. Christian Johnstone, although Cardigan was separated from
2 CONC  his wife since 1837. Criticism from her father caused her to leave home in 1857. After a period in a hotel, she took a furnished house in Norfolk Street, Park Lane, and became the Earl of Cardigan's mistress. After the Earl's wif
2 CONC e died in July, 1858, the couple sailed away together on the Earl's yacht and married in Gibraltar on September 28, 1858. She was shunned by polite society, but was an accomplished musician and horsewoman, and acknowledged as a le
2 CONC ading courtesan. She was left a life interest in the Cardigan estates on her husband's death in March, 1868.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been acquainted with Benjamin Disraeli for many year, the dowager Countess Cardigan wrote to him in early 1873 to suggest marriage. He broke off their correspondence, and only a few months later she was remarried on August 
2 CONC 28, 1873 to a Portuguese nobleman, Don António Manuel de Saldanha e Lancastre, who was created conde and visconde de Lancastre by Luís I of Portugal on September 11, 1873. Against usual custom, she merged her former title as an En
2 CONC glish dowager countess with her new title as wife of a Portuguese conde, and styled herself the Countess of Cardigan and Lancastre. The couple initially lived together in Portugal and Paris, but lived apart after Adeline returned 
2 CONC to Deene Park, the Cardigan ancestral home, in 1879 until the Conde's death in 1898, spending her way through the Cardigan family's fortune with her extravagances. Her title as Countess of Lancastre caused embarrassment to Queen V
2 CONC ictoria, who liked to travel incognito in Europe as "Countess of Lancaster."
2 CONT
2 CONT She became more eccentric in old age. As a widow, she scandalised society by wearing thick make-up and organizing steeplechases through the local graveyard "and became everyone's idea of a merry widow. She kept her coffin in the h
2 CONC ouse, she would often lie in it, asking for opinions on her appearance. Eventually her profligate spending led to bankruptcy which forced the sale of many of her clothes, carriages and horses. She was often seen, locally, cycling 
2 CONC clad in her first husband's regimental trousers.[3] She smoked cigarettes in public at a time when it was considered improper for a lady to smoke in private. She died at Deene Park and was buried near her first husband in the Deen
2 CONC e parish church. A character who may have been very loosely based on her was portrayed in the 1968 film, The Charge of the Light Brigade.
2 CONT
2 CONT in, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adeline,_Countess_of_Cardigan_and_Lancastre
